        "title": "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "5decc408-adb2-55a3-accb-8195735690a9",
        ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=\u27e80,\\,x\u27e9$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=\u27e8{e}^{y},\\,y\u27e9$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=\u27e8-\\frac{y}{5}+\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x,\\,\\frac{4x}{5}\u27e9$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=\u27e8P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)\u27e9,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={\u222c}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222e}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=\\frac{4}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
